INSERT INTO question VALUES('chem-5-3-20','chem','written',118,19,NULL,'Write a balanced equation for the reaction of 1-bromopropane with aqueous sodium hydroxide.','CH₃CH₂CH₂Br + NaOH → CH₃CH₂CH₂OH + NaBr (or CH₃CH₂CH₂Br + OH⁻ → CH₃CH₂CH₂OH + Br⁻).','This is a substitution producing propan-1-ol.',0,NULL,NULL,0,2,2.5,NULL,NULL,'original','unknown',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Imported from index.html (written for this site; some questions were drafted with AI help and then reviewed).','2026-11-18T08:22:45','2026-11-18T08:23:12');
INSERT INTO question VALUES('chem-5-3-21','chem','written',118,20,NULL,'Explain why alkenes undergo addition reactions but alkanes do not.','Alkenes are unsaturated, containing a C=C double bond that can open to bond to added atoms; alkanes are saturated with only single bonds, so they can only undergo substitution.','Reference to the C=C double bond/unsaturation is essential.',0,NULL,NULL,0,2,2.5,NULL,NULL,'original','unknown',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Imported from index.html (written for this site; some questions were drafted with AI help and then reviewed).','2026-11-18T08:22:45','2026-11-18T08:23:12');
INSERT INTO question VALUES('chem-5-3-22','chem','written',118,21,NULL,'Explain how the conditions of oxidising ethanol with acidified dichromate determine whether ethanal or ethanoic acid is the main product.','Distilling the ethanal off as it forms (limited oxidant) stops further oxidation, giving ethanal; heating under reflux with excess oxidant oxidises it fully to ethanoic acid.','Ethanal is an intermediate in the oxidation of a primary alcohol.',0,NULL,NULL,0,2,2.5,NULL,NULL,'original','unknown',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Imported from index.html (written for this site; some questions were drafted with AI help and then reviewed).','2026-11-18T08:22:45','2026-11-18T08:23:12');
INSERT INTO question VALUES('chem-5-3-23','chem','written',118,22,NULL,'What is the atom economy for producing ethanol by the hydration of ethene? Explain.','100%, because it is an addition reaction in which all reactant atoms end up in the single desired product.','Atom economy = mass of desired product ÷ total mass of reactants × 100.',0,NULL,NULL,0,2,2.5,NULL,NULL,'original','unknown',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Imported from index.html (written for this site; some questions were drafted with AI help and then reviewed).','2026-11-18T08:22:45','2026-11-18T08:23:12');
INSERT INTO question VALUES('chem-5-3-24','chem','written',118,23,NULL,'Calculate the atom economy for producing ethanol from chloroethane and sodium hydroxide: C₂H₅Cl + NaOH → C₂H₅OH + NaCl. (M: C₂H₅Cl 64.5, NaOH 40.0, C₂H₅OH 46.0 g/mol)','Atom economy = 46.0 ÷ (64.5 + 40.0) × 100 = 46.0 ÷ 104.5 × 100 ≈ 44.0%.','The NaCl by-product lowers the atom economy compared with hydration of ethene.',0,NULL,NULL,0,2,2.5,NULL,NULL,'original','unknown',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Imported from index.html (written for this site; some questions were drafted with AI help and then reviewed).','2026-11-18T08:22:45','2026-11-18T08:23:12');
INSERT INTO question VALUES('chem-5-3-25','chem','written',118,24,NULL,'Describe a simple chemical test to distinguish hexene from hexane.','Add bromine water: hexene rapidly decolourises it (orange/brown to colourless) by addition across the C=C; hexane does not decolourise it quickly.','Alkanes only react with bromine slowly in UV light.',0,NULL,NULL,0,2,2.5,NULL,NULL,'original','unknown',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Imported from index.html (written for this site; some questions were drafted with AI help and then reviewed).','2026-11-18T08:22:45','2026-11-18T08:23:12');
